  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "🏺 Ancient Greek Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Real Greek names with their meanings, the way Greeks used them: one name, a father and a city."
    },
    "intro": "Ancient Greeks had one name, and it usually meant something: Nikias is victory, Philippos is lover of horses, Theodoros is gift of god. To tell people apart they added their father's name or their city. This **Ancient Greek name generator** uses real Greek names, shows their meanings, and builds full names the Greek way.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How Greek names worked",
        "content": "<p>A Greek was known by a single personal name. Formally, a citizen added their father's name and, in Athens, their deme (district): the historian Thucydides introduces himself as Thucydides son of Olorus, an Athenian. Outside their city, Greeks were known by their home: Herodotus of Halicarnassus, Thales of Miletus.</p><p>Many names were built from two meaningful parts, just like <a href=\"anglo-saxon-name-generator.html\">Anglo-Saxon</a> ones: <em>Nik-</em> (victory), <em>-andros</em> (man), <em>Hipp-</em> (horse), <em>Theo-</em> (god), <em>-kles</em> (glory).</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Reading the meanings",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Horses</strong> were a sign of wealth, so aristocratic families loved names with <em>hippos</em>: Philippos, Xanthippos, Hipparete.</li><li><strong>Gods</strong> appear in names like Demetrios (of Demeter) and Diodoros (gift of Zeus).</li><li><strong>Virtues and victory</strong>: Nikias, Aristides, Agathon.</li></ul><p>Names are given in their Greek forms (Philippos rather than Philip); the meanings are the usual translations of their parts.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Using the names",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>For a citizen</strong>, use the father's name format: it's how they'd sign a decree.</li><li><strong>For a traveller</strong>, use the city: away from home, where you came from was what mattered.</li><li>For the Romans, see the <a href=\"roman-name-generator.html\">Roman Name Generator</a>.</li></ul>"
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
